    {
      "text": "Here is a comprehensive background overview for your syllabus — drawing on what the tools returned plus well-established scholarly knowledge about this landmark novel:\n\n---\n\n## *To Kill a Mockingbird* — Harper Lee (1960)\n### Background for an American Literature Survey Course\n\n---\n\n### 📖 Publication History\n\n- **Published:** July 11, 1960, by **J.B. Lippincott & Co.**, New York\n- **ISBN:** 978-0-06-112008-4 (current HarperCollins edition)\n- **Word count:** ~100,000 words\n- The novel grew out of Lee's experiences growing up in Monroeville, Alabama, and is widely understood as semi-autobiographical — Scout Finch is thought to be based on Lee herself, and neighbor Charles Baker \"Dill\" Harris on her childhood friend **Truman Capote**.\n- Lee reportedly worked on the manuscript for two and a half years with guidance from editor **Tay Hohoff** at Lippincott, substantially reworking early drafts.\n- It became an **immediate bestseller**, spending 88 weeks on the *New York Times* Best Seller list and selling over **40 million copies** worldwide to date — one of the best-selling American novels ever published.\n- It won the **Pulitzer Prize for Fiction in 1961**, just one year after publication — an extraordinarily fast recognition for the award.\n- A celebrated **film adaptation** directed by Robert Mulligan, starring **Gregory Peck** as Atticus Finch, was released in 1962. Peck won the Academy Award for Best Actor, and the film is now considered a classic.\n- A **sequel/companion novel**, *Go Set a Watchman*, was published in 2015. Originally written before *Mockingbird*, it was long thought lost and its publication was controversial (see below).\n\n---\n\n### 📰 Critical Reception\n\n- **Initial reviews** were largely enthusiastic. Critics praised Lee's moral clarity, her vivid evocation of 1930s Alabama, and the freshness of Scout's child narrator voice.\n- The novel was celebrated for addressing **racial injustice and the failure of the American legal system** at precisely the moment the Civil Rights Movement was gaining momentum — the timing gave it enormous cultural resonance.\n- **Atticus Finch** became one of the most celebrated characters in American fiction, widely held up as a model of moral courage and legal ethics. He was voted the **#1 greatest hero in American film history** by the American Film Institute (for Gregory Peck's portrayal).\n- Over the decades, the novel became a **cornerstone of American secondary and university curricula**, read by tens of millions of students.\n- Later critical reassessments (especially from the 1990s onward) raised more complex and challenging readings — see controversies below.\n\n---\n\n### ⚡ Major Controversies\n\n**1. Censorship and Banning (1960s–present)**\nThe novel has been **frequently challenged and banned** in schools and libraries across the United States, making it perennially one of the most banned books in America. Objections have come from multiple directions:\n- **Language:** The novel uses racial slurs (including the N-word) extensively and authentically in its historical context.\n- **Content:** Some school districts have cited its themes of rape and racial violence as inappropriate for younger readers.\n- Notable recent bans include a **Biloxi, Mississippi school district** removing it from 8th-grade reading lists in 2017, citing the racial language.\n\n**2. The \"White Savior\" Critique**\nBeginning in earnest in the 1990s and intensifying in more recent decades, scholars and critics — many of them African American — have questioned the novel's racial politics:\n- The story centers on **white characters' perspectives** (Scout, Atticus) rather than on Tom Robinson, the Black man whose fate drives the plot. Tom is largely passive and voiceless.\n- Critics argue the novel offers white readers **moral comfort** — allowing them to identify with heroic white liberalism — without truly centering Black experience or agency.\n- Scholar **Toni Morrison** and others explored how American literature often uses Black characters as backdrop for white moral development rather than as full subjects in their own right.\n- **Monroe Freedman's** 1992 essay \"Atticus Finch, Esq., R.I.P.\" was a landmark legal-ethics critique arguing Atticus was not the moral hero lawyers had idealized.\n\n**3. *Go Set a Watchman* (2015) and Its Fallout**\n- When *Go Set a Watchman* was published, it revealed an **older Atticus Finch** who holds racist views and attends a Ku Klux Klan meeting. This shocked many readers and destabilized decades of hero-worship around the character.\n- The publication itself was controversial: Lee's literary agent and publisher were accused of taking advantage of an **elderly, infirm author** (Lee had suffered a stroke and had impaired hearing and vision). Lee's longtime lawyer Alice Lee, her protector, had died months before the deal was announced. Many questioned whether Harper Lee had truly given informed consent.\n- Critics debated whether *Watchman* should be read as a **\"first draft\"** (which it technically was) or as a legitimate companion text that recontextualizes *Mockingbird*.\n\n**4. Authorship Speculation**\nA persistent fringe theory holds that **Truman Capote** wrote or substantially co-wrote *To Kill a Mockingbird*. Scholars have largely dismissed this, though Lee did acknowledge Capote's friendship and influence. The debate has resurfaced periodically.\n\n---\n\n### 🎓 Key Themes for Your Syllabus\n\n| Theme | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| **Racial Injustice** | Central to the Tom Robinson trial; historically resonant with Jim Crow |\n| **Moral Courage** | Atticus as model — and later, as a flawed one |\n| **Childhood & Innocence** | Scout's coming-of-age; loss of innocence as American trope |\n| **Southern Gothic** | Maycomb's social decay, Boo Radley, Gothic atmosphere |\n| **Class & Social Hierarchy** | The Finches, Cunninghams, Ewells as social strata |\n| **Empathy** | Atticus's famous instruction to \"climb into someone's skin\" |\n\n---\n\n### 📚 Suggested Pairing Texts for Your Syllabus\n- *Go Set a Watchman* (Harper Lee, 2015) — for comparative study\n- *Native Son* (Richard Wright, 1940) — contemporaneous Black perspective on race and justice\n- *The Help* (Kathryn Stockett, 2009) — for white savior narrative comparison\n- Essays by **Toni Morrison** (*Playing in the Dark*, 1992) — on race in American literature\n- Monroe Freedman's essay on Atticus Finch for law and ethics discussions\n\n---\n\nThis should give you a strong foundation for contextualizing the novel in your survey course. Let me know if you'd like deeper dives into any particular aspect — critical essays, the film adaptation, or contemporary classroom approaches!",
      "mediaUrl": null
    }
