      "text": "Those pages are not accessible to me directly. I want to be straightforward with you rather than guess or fabricate results. Here is an honest, accurate answer based on well-established facts about this novel's copyright and distribution — and some practical guidance for your situation:\n\n---\n\n## Digital Access to *To Kill a Mockingbird* — What You Need to Know\n\n### ❌ Not in the Public Domain — No Free Open Access\n\n*To Kill a Mockingbird* (1960) is **firmly under copyright** and will remain so for the foreseeable future. Harper Lee died in 2016, meaning under U.S. copyright law the work is protected for **70 years after the author's death** — until at least **2086**. It is **not available** on:\n- **Project Gutenberg** (public domain only)\n- **Internet Archive** free lending (the novel's rightsholder has actively enforced copyright)\n- Any legitimate open-access repository\n\nAny site offering the full text for free is doing so **illegally**, and you should not direct students there.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Where Licensed Digital Access *May* Be Available\n\nThe availability of a licensed ebook through your institution will depend entirely on your library's specific subscriptions. Here are the platforms worth checking with your librarian:\n\n| Platform | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| **OverDrive / Libby** | Most university and public libraries license ebooks here; simultaneous-user limits often apply |\n| **ProQuest Ebook Central** | Common in academic libraries; check if your institution has a HarperCollins fiction license |\n| **EBSCO eBook Collection** | Similar to ProQuest; fiction coverage varies widely by institution |\n| **Kanopy** | Primarily film/video, but some text content; less likely for this title |\n| **VitalSource / Chegg** | Student-facing ebook rental platforms; the novel is available for individual purchase/rental here |\n\n**Important caveat:** Even where the ebook exists on these platforms, **simultaneous user limits** are a serious practical problem for course adoption. A library may own 1–3 licenses, which is fine for individual borrowing but not for a whole class reading at once.\n\n---\n\n### 📚 Practical Recommendations for Your Course\n\nGiven the copyright situation, here are your realistic options ranked by practicality:\n\n1. **Physical paperback (most reliable):** The **HarperCollins Perennial Modern Classics** paperback (ISBN: 978-0-06-112008-4) is widely available, inexpensive (~$9–$14 retail, often less used), and has no access or simultaneous-use issues. This is by far the most common course adoption format.\n\n2. **Consult your campus librarian directly:** Ask specifically whether your library holds a multi-user or unlimited-access ebook license for this title. Some institutions have negotiated broader licenses. Your librarian can also potentially pursue an **institutional ebook purchase** in advance of your course if given enough lead time.\n\n3. **Publisher's ebook / student rental:** If you want a digital option, directing students to purchase or rent the ebook individually via **Amazon Kindle**, **Apple Books**, **Google Play Books**, or **VitalSource** is straightforward and affordable (~$8–$13). This doesn't require library coordination.\n\n4. **Course reserves:** Your library can place a limited number of **physical copies on course reserve** for students who can't afford to purchase — a useful supplement.\n\n5. **What you *cannot* do:** You cannot legally include the novel's full text in a digital course pack or post it to your LMS. Photocopying the entire novel for a course pack would also require licensing through the **Copyright Clearance Center (CCC)**, which is typically cost-prohibitive for a full novel.\n\n---\n\n### 🔑 Bottom Line\n\n**There is no free, legal, full-text digital version of this novel.** For a course adoption, the **physical paperback remains your most practical, equitable, and reliable choice** — it's cheap, universally accessible, and requires no technology or login. If digital is a priority, your best path is to contact your campus library now (before the semester) to explore multi-user ebook licensing, or direct students to affordable individual ebook purchase/rental.\n\nWould you like help identifying critical essays or secondary sources that *are* freely available through open-access databases like JSTOR or EBSCO, which you could pair with a physical copy of the novel?",
