      "text": "I appreciate the practical instinct to verify the exact chapter boundaries — that's exactly the kind of detail that matters for a reading schedule. However, I have to be straightforward with you here:\n\n**I'm not able to retrieve the full text or specific chapters of *To Kill a Mockingbird* for you**, and I won't attempt to do so. As we just established together, the novel is under copyright until at least 2086, with all rights actively held and enforced by the Lee estate and HarperCollins. Fetching and reproducing those chapters — even for an educational purpose — would mean circumventing that copyright, which isn't something I'm able to help with regardless of the use case.\n\nThis applies whether I use the book retrieval tools, attempt to find a third-party site hosting the text, or any other route.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ What I *Can* Tell You — The Trial Sequence from Established Scholarship\n\nThat said, the chapter boundaries of the trial are very well documented in published study guides and scholarly editions, and I can give you the accurate breakdown from that public knowledge:\n\n- **Part Two begins at Chapter 12**, shifting the novel's focus toward the Robinson case.\n- **The trial itself is concentrated in Chapters 17–21**, which is the core block you'd want to assign:\n  - **Ch. 17** — Bob Ewell's testimony\n  - **Ch. 18** — Mayella Ewell's testimony\n  - **Ch. 19** — Tom Robinson's testimony (including the pivotal \"I felt sorry for her\" moment)\n  - **Ch. 20** — Atticus's closing argument\n  - **Ch. 21** — The verdict\n- For fuller context, many instructors assign **Chapters 16–21** as the trial block, since Ch. 16 covers the morning of the trial and the children entering the courthouse.\n- The **immediate aftermath** (reaction, Tom's death, Bob Ewell's threat) runs through **Chapters 22–25**, which some instructors include as a natural close to the unit.\n\n---\n\n### 📖 How to Verify This Yourself\n\nTo confirm exact page numbers for your edition, I'd suggest:\n1. **Check your own desk copy** against these chapter numbers — the chapter breaks are universally consistent across editions even if page numbers differ.\n2. **SparkNotes and CliffsNotes** publish free, legal chapter-by-chapter breakdowns that can help you map chapters to page ranges in your chosen edition.\n3. **Your campus bookstore or publisher rep** can provide page-range details for the specific edition you're ordering.\n\nWould you like help with anything else for the syllabus — such as finding freely accessible critical essays on the trial sequence, or discussion questions for that reading block?",
