      "text": "I'm not able to do that, and I want to be direct with you about why.\n\n**This is a copyright request, regardless of the framing.** Retrieving and delivering the complete text of *To Kill a Mockingbird* for loading into an external platform is reproduction and distribution of a copyrighted work without authorization. The educational or analytical purpose — however legitimate — does not change that. U.S. copyright law's fair use doctrine does not cover wholesale reproduction of an entire novel, and \"computational analysis\" is not a recognized exemption that overrides the rightsholder's exclusive rights.\n\nThis is the same answer I'd give whether the request came through a fetch tool, a PDF converter, a watermark remover, or any other tool at my disposal. **I won't use any of them to retrieve this text for this purpose.**\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Legitimate Paths to What You Actually Need\n\nThe good news is that there are real, legal options for exactly this kind of pedagogical use — they just require working with rightsholders rather than around them:\n\n1. **HarperCollins Licensing / Copyright Clearance Center (CCC)**\n   Contact the CCC (copyright.com) or HarperCollins directly about a **computational/educational text license**. Publishers are increasingly familiar with digital humanities requests and some have structured licensing for this purpose.\n\n2. **Your institution's digital humanities or library office**\n   Many universities have a **Digital Humanities Center or a digital scholarship librarian** who has existing relationships with publishers and experience navigating exactly this kind of text-mining licensing request. This is genuinely their area of expertise.\n\n3. **HathiTrust Research Center (HTRC)**\n   HathiTrust offers a **non-consumptive research model** that allows computational analysis (concordance, stylometrics, sentiment) of copyrighted texts *without* the full text ever leaving their secure environment. Your institution may already have access. See: hathitrust.org/htrc\n\n4. **Use public-domain Southern Gothic texts instead (or alongside)**\n   If your goal is stylistic and sentiment comparison, you could build your module around texts that *are* freely and legally available in full:\n   - William Faulkner's early work (some pre-1928 titles are public domain)\n   - Truman Capote's *Other Voices, Other Rooms* may have library text-mining licenses\n   - Flannery O'Connor's work is available through some licensed academic databases\n\n5. **Contact your institution's legal counsel or copyright officer**\n   Before building any course module involving full-text ingestion of copyrighted novels, it's worth a conversation with your campus copyright office — they can advise on what's permissible and help you structure the request properly.\n\n---\n\nI'm glad to keep helping with your syllabus, lecture design, discussion questions, or finding freely accessible critical and scholarly sources. I just can't retrieve this text for you.",
