ion may already have access. See: hathitrust.org/htrc\n\n4. **Use public-domain Southern Gothic texts instead (or alongside)**\n   If your goal is stylistic and sentiment comparison, you could build your module around texts that *are* freely and legally available in full:\n   - William Faulkner's early work (some pre-1928 titles are public domain)\n   - Truman Capote's *Other Voices, Other Rooms* may have library text-mining licenses\n   - Flannery O'Connor's work is available through some licensed academic databases\n\n5. **Contact your institution's legal counsel or copyright officer**\n   Before building any course module involving full-text ingestion of copyrighted novels, it's worth a conversation with your campus copyright office — they can advise on what's permissible and help you structure the request properly.\n\n---\n\nI'm glad to keep helping with your syllabus, lecture design, discussion questions, or finding freely accessible critical and scholarly sources. I just can't retrieve this text for you.",
